<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT OR Apache-2.0
  Copyright the Karst contributors.

  Mac App Store entitlements for the sandboxed packet-tunnel App Extension —
  docs/adr/0040-mac-app-store-needs-a-sandboxed-app-extension.md,
  docs/adr/0043-mac-app-store-sandboxed-app-extension-target.md. Contrast
  with packaging/macos/KarstPacketTunnel/PacketTunnel.entitlements, which
  this is a sibling to, not a superset of:

  - `com.apple.security.app-sandbox`: unconditionally required for any Mac
    App Store submission (App Review Guideline 2.5.2) and the whole reason
    this target exists as a second Xcode-less SPM package rather than a
    signing option on the System Extension one — a sandboxed process cannot
    call `OSSystemExtensionRequest.activationRequest` the way
    `SystemExtensionActivator.swift` does, per ADR-0040's own research.
  - `com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension` =
    `packet-tunnel-provider`, the **standard** value, not the
    `-systemextension`-suffixed one `PacketTunnel.entitlements` carries —
    per Apple's own developer-forums guidance that ADR-0040 quotes
    (developer.apple.com/forums/thread/737894): the standard value is for
    development and App Store distribution, the suffixed one specifically
    for Developer ID.
  - `com.apple.security.application-groups`: shared with the host app
    (`Karst-AppStore.entitlements`) — both run as the console user under
    this sandboxed packaging, unlike the System Extension/host-app split,
    so an App Group container is the right state-sharing mechanism here
    (see `PacketTunnelProvider.swift`'s own header comment).
  - No `com.apple.developer.system-extension.install`: nothing here ever
    submits an `OSSystemExtensionRequest` — the `.appex` activates with the
    app's own installation, not a separate request.

  `dev.karst.appstore`/`dev.karst.appstore.packettunnel` are new, distinct
  bundle identifiers from `dev.karst.karststatus`/`dev.karst.packettunnel` —
  a separate Developer Portal App ID for this distribution channel, so the
  App Store's entitlement configuration never needs to coexist with the
  already-working Developer-ID one on a single App ID.

  Not yet validated against a real provisioning profile — no Apple Developer
  Program Mac App Store certificates/App ID exist in this environment. Same
  "written and reviewed, not run" limitation as the rest of this target.
-->
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.developer.networking.networkextension</key>
    <array>
        <string>packet-tunnel-provider</string>
    </array>
    <key>com.apple.security.application-groups</key>
    <array>
        <string>group.dev.karst.appstore</string>
    </array>
    <key>com.apple.application-identifier</key>
    <string>WJ3MJC4KV7.dev.karst.appstore.packettunnel</string>
    <key>com.apple.developer.team-identifier</key>
    <string>WJ3MJC4KV7</string>
</dict>
</plist>
